# Upstream openclaw skills to KEEP in the built image.
#
# THIS LIST IS EMPTY, ON PURPOSE.
#
# The bot has exactly four skills, all of them defined in this repository and
# mounted from ConfigMaps in k8s/: `developer`, `tester`, `reviewer` and
# `projects`. Those are the ones it is supposed to have, and they are the only
# ones anybody reviewed. Everything openclaw bundles at /app/skills — around
# fifty of them, from 1password and apple-notes to spotify-player and trello —
# is removed at build time.
#
# WHY AN ALLOWLIST AND NOT A DENYLIST
# -----------------------------------
# This started as a denylist of three: clawhub, skill-creator and mcporter.
# A denylist has the wrong default. It only stops what somebody already
# thought of, every upstream release can add capabilities that arrive switched
# on, and nothing about that is visible in a diff of this repository — the
# image just quietly grows. An allowlist inverts it: a new bundled skill is
# removed by default, and keeping one is a line in this file that a human has
# to write and a reviewer gets to see.
#
# The three that prompted it are the sharpest examples, and worth naming
# because they explain what "capability" means here:
#
#   clawhub        The skill marketplace client. Its own description: "Search
#                  ClawHub for skills when a requested capability is not
#                  already available; install, verify, update, publish, or
#                  sync skills." It downloads other people's skills into the
#                  running agent.
#   skill-creator  Guides the agent through authoring new SKILL.md files.
#   mcporter       Dynamic MCP-server gateway — loads tool servers over
#                  HTTP/stdio at runtime, routing around the static MCP
#                  registration in openclaw.json, which is the list that
#                  decides what tools exist at all.
#
# A skill is instructions the agent will follow, so a skill it acquires by
# itself can tell it to do anything the sandbox permits — including working
# around the allowed-projects permission list. That is why the bar is "did
# this go through a pull request", not "does it look harmful".
#
# NOTE — removing a skill removes INSTRUCTIONS, not the ability behind them.
# `openclaw skills install` is a CLI and stays in the image; the agent's exec
# tool can call it. What actually stops an install is that its destination
# directories are root-owned and read-only — see SKILLS-DIR-READONLY in
# k8s/020-deployment.yaml. This file and that lockdown are both needed.
#
# TO KEEP ONE: add its directory name on its own line, and say why. It will
# be loadable by the agent from then on.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Skills also ship INSIDE extensions, not just in /app/skills:
#   /app/extensions/<ext>/skills/<name>/    (and mirrored under /app/dist/)
# That second location only came to light when the strip was widened and the
# build failed on six of them. Four belong to extensions that are not in
# openclaw.json's plugins.allow — open-prose, imessage, memory-wiki — so they
# were inert weight and are removed. The two below belong to extensions that
# ARE enabled, and they are kept deliberately:

# The deployment tester's core loop is browser work: open the deployed app,
# complete an Entra sign-in, smoke-test the flows
# (builder/tester-runner.sh). "browser" is in plugins.allow, so the plugin — the tooling — is
# enabled either way; this skill is the instructions for driving it. Dropping
# it would leave the tool without its guidance, and whether TOOLS-entra.md and
# the runner prompts cover the gap is not something a build can answer.
browser-automation

# "canvas" is likewise in plugins.allow. Kept for the same reason: an enabled
# plugin should keep the instructions that come with it.
canvas
